    "turn_text": "Whether brought to you by 1st National Bank of North Platte, the bank you call 1st, 4 tonight, partly cloudy skies in a low around 25, tomorrow partly cloudy in a high around 45, tomorrow night cloudy, 40% chance of rain and snow in a low around 25, Friday partly cloudy with highs in the low to mid 30s, then Friday night mostly clear in a low around 15, Saturday mostly sunny with highs in the 30s lows in the teens, then Sunday mostly sunny with highs in the 40s lows 15 to 25 in downtown. We've got barometer at 29.9 and rising. Little or no wins, partly cloudy skies and it's 42 degrees in Shadron. Whether brought to you by First National Bank of North Platt, click on the First National Bank of North Platt weather button on the upper right corner of the Shadron home page and it will take you to the First National North Platt website. News today brought to you by Northwest Rural Public Power District of Hay Springs, your touch-tone energy partner. A bill that boosts the list of controlled substances in South Dakota has been passed by a Senate committee. Lawmakers vote 6-1 on the bill, which goes next to the Senate floor. If the bill becomes law, anyone caught possessing or selling synthetic marijuana or bath salt drugs could face a felony charge. Panel members say South Dakota has an epidemic on its hands, given soaring numbers of emergency room admissions. Some health committee members say the drugs affects are more severe than regular marijuana and should come off the shelves. Criminal defense lawyer Bob Peacell says a felony conviction is too extreme as it has a permanent impact on a person's life. Senator Bruce Rampleberg agrees saying that young people do stupid things. The Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City says strong farm income in Midwestern western states improved agricultural banks balance sheets but reduced demand for new loans. The Federal released a new report on ag credit conditions on Wednesday. The Fed said bank profits in the region improved in the third quarter of 2011 because many borrowers paid down some of their farm debts. Many farmers also use proceeds from last fall's high crop prices to pay operating costs, so demand for operating loans fell. The 10th Federal Reserve District in Kansas City covers Kansas, Nebraska, Oklahoma, Wyoming, Colorado, northern New Mexico, and western Missouri. South Dakota lawmakers are looking at a proposal to establish a funding goal for school districts. The bill's main sponsor is House Democratic Leader Bernie Huttonhoff of Yankton, who argues that the state is not giving school districts enough financial aid. The bill would establish a funding goal based on average spending per student in South Dakota and neighboring states. An annual report would indicate how much South Dakota is falling short of that spending goal. The House State Affairs Committee originally planned to discuss the bill today, But the panel delayed a hearing on the measure until next week. Members of the Democratic minority have said the state should increase aid to schools by more than Republican Governor Dennis Dugard has recommended. And Bill designed to limit the spread of black-tailed prairie dogs in Nebraska, his one first round approval in the legislature. Lawmakers voted 30 to nothing today to advance the measure by Ellsworth's Senator, Leroy Loudon. The proposal would give counties the power to carry out a management program to help control the animals, which ranchers often view as pests. The bill is intended to prevent black-tailed prairie dog colonies from expanding from one person's property to another. Counties would have the power to notify landowners that a colony is not being managed and could require landowners to take action. Landowners would also have to notify counties that they have moved to address the problem. The bill requires two more votes before it goes to Governor Dave Heinemann. That's a look at news brought to you by the Northwest Rural Public Power District of Hay Springs, your touchstone energy partner.",
