      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 84506,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84506.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 164,
    "turn_text": "And then kind of dovetailing with the issue of lobbyists is you have several points in here about increasing transparency. That's something that in recent years we've heard a lot from President Obama, from Nancy Pelosi. We've heard about how it's going to be the most transparent government ever and then nothing really changes. So how do you go about making government more transparent?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_06",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "There are multiple points in the document regarding increased transparency.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The claim was made that the current administration would establish the most transparent government ever.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Despite promises, little to no actual change has occurred in government transparency.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "President Obama has advocated for greater government transparency.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Nancy Pelosi has advocated for greater government transparency.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "In recent years, there has been public discourse emphasizing transparency in government.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Transparency is a desirable goal for government operations.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Public officials' stated commitments to transparency should be expected to result in tangible reforms.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The failure to deliver on transparency promises undermines trust in political leadership.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Audiences expect consistency between rhetoric and policy outcomes in governance.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Transparency improvements require deliberate institutional action rather than symbolic declarations.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 84506,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84506.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 165,
    "turn_text": "Well, there's a number of things we can do and get some Nebraska common sense to Washington. Here in Nebraska, I serve in a body where we have public hearings. And that's what we're doing right now in the afternoon. We're having public hearings. Any citizen can come in and they can testify on a bill. Every bill has a hearing. So we need to look at things like that before we, or when we need to make those changes in the US Senate. First of all, I think senators should be reading the bills. And it should be present for debate. Sometimes when you are watching debate of the Senate, no one's there. And these are big policy issues that affect all of our lives. And so we need to require that senators be present and that they do a lot of significant amount for debate on these major pieces of legislation. For example, Obamacare. There wasn't a lot of debate on it. Amendments weren't accepted. That means to change. We have to quit playing these games. We need to do what's right for the people of this country. And it's important that we make this.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
